In C++, classes can define their own memory management functions by overloading the global or class-specific operator new and operator delete. This allows fine-grained control over how memory is allocated and deallocated for objects, enabling optimizations, debugging, or specialized memory handling strategies. Modern C++ encourages explicit control over resource management, and custom operators are a key feature for implementing patterns like memory pooling or tracking allocations.


**Syntax:**
void* operator new(std::size_t size); 
void operator delete(void* ptr) noexcept;
// For class-specific operators:
ReturnType* ClassName::operator new(std::size_t size);
