Expression templates are a powerful C++ metaprogramming technique that allows for the compile-time evaluation of expressions, significantly improving performance, particularly in numerical computation.  They avoid the creation of temporary objects during expression evaluation by representing expressions as abstract syntax trees (ASTs) at compile time.  The compiler then optimizes the expression directly into efficient machine code, often eliminating unnecessary intermediate results and memory allocations. This contrasts sharply with traditional operator overloading, which typically involves the creation of temporary objects for each operation.  The implementation relies heavily on template specialization and recursion to build and evaluate the AST at compile time.


**Syntax:**
The core involves creating a template class that represents an expression.  This class usually overloads operators to create nested expressions, building the AST recursively.  A final specialization evaluates the expression by recursively unwrapping the AST.  This often involves techniques like tag dispatching or SFINAE to control evaluation paths.  Consider this simplified example of vector addition:


**Example:**
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
template <typename T>
class Expression {};
template <typename T>
class VectorExpression : public Expression<T> {
  const std::vector<T>& vec;
public:
  VectorExpression(const std::vector<T>& v) : vec(v) {}
};
template <typename T>
class AddExpression : public Expression<T> {
  const Expression<T>& left;
  const Expression<T>& right;
public:
  AddExpression(const Expression<T>& l, const Expression<T>& r) : left(l), right(r) {}
};
template <typename T>
std::vector<T> evaluate(const Expression<T>& expr) {
  // Recursive evaluation logic based on the expression type
  if (auto* vecExpr = dynamic_cast<const VectorExpression<T>*>(&expr)) {
    return vecExpr->vec;
  }
  if (auto* addExpr = dynamic_cast<const AddExpression<T>*>(&expr)) {
    std::vector<T> leftResult = evaluate(*addExpr->left);
    std::vector<T> rightResult = evaluate(*addExpr->right);
      //perform elementwise addition
    std::vector<T> result(leftResult.size());
    for(size_t i = 0; i < leftResult.size(); ++i){
        result[i] = leftResult[i] + rightResult[i];
    }
    return result;
  }
  throw std::runtime_error("Unknown expression type");
}
int main() {
  std::vector<double> v1 = {1.0, 2.0, 3.0};
  std::vector<double> v2 = {4.0, 5.0, 6.0};
  auto expr = AddExpression<double>(VectorExpression<double>(v1), VectorExpression<double>(v2));
  auto result = evaluate(expr);
  for (double val : result) std::cout << val << " "; // Output: 5 7 9
  std::cout << std::endl;
  return 0;
}
```

