```

This example demonstrates advanced compile-time polymorphism using `std::result_of` (or `std::invoke_result` in C++20 and later) and perfect forwarding within a custom random number generator (RNG) interface.  It highlights how the compiler deduces return types at compile time, avoiding runtime overhead, and showcases the importance of perfect forwarding to maintain flexibility in argument passing.  The compiler's implementation leverages template metaprogramming to generate specialized code for different RNG types and their associated return types, thus optimizing performance by eliminating runtime type checking and dispatch.


**Syntax:**
```cpp
template<class URNG>
struct MyDistribution {
  using result_type = decltype(std::declval<URNG&>()()); // C++11/14
  //using result_type = std::invoke_result_t<URNG&>; // C++20 and later

  template<typename... Args>
  auto operator()(URNG& g, Args&&... args) -> std::result_of_t<URNG&()> {
    // Perfect forwarding ensures the URNG's operator() is called with the correct arguments
    return g(std::forward<Args>(args)...);
  }
};
```


**Example:**
