RAII is a fundamental C++ programming technique that tightly couples resource management with object lifetime.  It ensures that resources (like dynamically allocated memory, files, network connections, etc.) are automatically acquired when an object is created and released when the object is destroyed, even in the face of exceptions.  The Rule of Five dictates that if you define any of the special member functions (copy constructor, copy assignment operator, move constructor, move assignment operator, or destructor), you should explicitly define *all* of them to prevent subtle bugs and resource leaks.  Failing to do so can lead to double deletion, memory leaks, or other undefined behavior.


**Syntax:**
Example showing the Rule of Five implemented correctly:

```c++
class HasPtr {
public:
    HasPtr(const std::string& s = std::string{}) : ps(new std::string(s)), i(0) {}
    HasPtr(const HasPtr& other) : ps(new std::string(*other.ps)), i(other.i) {}
    HasPtr& operator=(const HasPtr& rhs) {
        if (this != &rhs) {
            delete ps;
            ps = new std::string(*rhs.ps);
            i = rhs.i;
        }
        return *this;
    }
    HasPtr(HasPtr&& other) noexcept : ps(other.ps), i(other.i) { other.ps = nullptr; }
    HasPtr& operator=(HasPtr&& rhs) noexcept {
        if (this != &rhs) {
            delete ps;
            ps = rhs.ps;
            i = rhs.i;
            rhs.ps = nullptr;
        }
        return *this;
    }
    ~HasPtr() { delete ps; }
    // ... other methods ...
private:
    std::string* ps;
    int i;
};
```

**Practical Usage Notes:**  The copy constructor creates a deep copy, avoiding shared ownership of the dynamically allocated string. The copy assignment operator handles self-assignment and performs a deep copy. The move constructor and move assignment operator efficiently transfer ownership of the dynamically allocated string, avoiding unnecessary copying. The destructor releases the dynamically allocated memory.  Modern C++ often leverages smart pointers (unique_ptr, shared_ptr) to simplify RAII, making manual memory management less error-prone.

